# Explanation bubbles: what each text says comes from the source its -source message names.
nutrition-help-what = What it is
nutrition-help-use = What it is for
nutrition-help-where = Where to find it
nutrition-help-source = Source: { $source }
nutrition-help-source-todo = Source: to be documented
nutrition-help-reference = Reference intake ({ $standard }): { $amount } per day.
nutrition-help-reference-none = No reference intake in the { $standard } standard.
nutrition-help-recipe = This recipe: { $amount } per serving.
nutrition-help-recipe-pct = This recipe: { $amount } per serving, { $pct } % of the reference intake.
nutrition-help-rating-traffic = UK traffic lights, per 100 g: good up to { $low } { $unit }, medium up to { $high } { $unit }, not good above.
nutrition-help-rating-dv-limit = To limit: { $low } % of the daily value or less is low, { $high } % or more is high.
nutrition-help-rating-dv-encourage = To get more of: { $high } % of the daily value or more is high.
nutrition-help-rating-dv-none = No daily value for this nutrient: the FDA does not rate it.
nutrition-help-rating-claim-max = EU claim “{ $name }”: up to { $amount } { $unit } per 100 g.
nutrition-help-rating-claim-min = EU claim “{ $name }”: from { $amount } { $unit } per 100 g.
nutrition-claim-low-fat = low in fat
nutrition-claim-low-saturated-fat = low in saturated fat
nutrition-claim-low-sugars = low in sugars
nutrition-claim-low-sodium = low in salt
nutrition-claim-source-of-fibre = source of fibre
nutrition-claim-high-fibre = high in fibre
nutrition-help-energy-source = Regulation (EU) 1169/2011, Annex XIV; NHS, Fat: the facts (2023)
nutrition-help-lipids-source = NHS, Fat: the facts (2023)
nutrition-help-saturated-fat-source = WHO, Healthy diet (2026); NHS, Fat: the facts (2023)
nutrition-help-unsaturated-fat-source = WHO, Healthy diet (2026)
nutrition-help-monounsaturated-fat-source = NHS, Fat: the facts (2023)
nutrition-help-polyunsaturated-fat-source = NHS, Fat: the facts (2023)
nutrition-help-omega-3-source = NHS, Fat: the facts (2023)
nutrition-help-omega-6-source = NHS, Fat: the facts (2023)
nutrition-help-trans-fat-source = WHO, Trans fat (2024); NHS, Fat: the facts (2023)
nutrition-help-cholesterol-source = NHS, Fat: the facts (2023)
nutrition-help-carbohydrates-source = WHO, Healthy diet (2026); Regulation (EU) 1169/2011, Annex I
nutrition-help-sugars-source = WHO, Healthy diet (2026); NHS, Sugar: the facts (2023); Regulation (EU) 1169/2011, Annex I
nutrition-help-starch-source = NHS, Starchy foods and carbohydrates (2023)
nutrition-help-fibers-source = WHO, Healthy diet (2026); NHS, How to get more fibre into your diet (2026)
nutrition-help-proteins-source = WHO, Healthy diet (2026)
nutrition-help-salt-source = WHO, Sodium reduction (2026); NHS, Salt in your diet (2023); Regulation (EU) 1169/2011, Annex I
nutrition-help-sodium-source = WHO, Sodium reduction (2026)
nutrition-help-energy-what = What the food gives the body, in kilocalories (kcal) or kilojoules (kJ).
nutrition-help-energy-use = It is counted with fixed factors: 9 kcal per gram of fat, 4 of carbohydrate and of protein, 7 of alcohol, 2 of fibre.
nutrition-help-lipids-what = The fats and oils of a food.
nutrition-help-lipids-use = Fat is a source of essential fatty acids, helps absorb vitamins A, D and E, and gives 9 kcal per gram, against 4 for carbohydrate and protein.
nutrition-help-saturated-fat-what = Fats that come mostly from animal sources, and from palm and coconut oil.
nutrition-help-saturated-fat-use = Too much raises the “bad” LDL cholesterol and the risk of heart disease and stroke. WHO: less than 10 % of the energy.
nutrition-help-saturated-fat-where = Fatty meat, sausages and pies, butter, ghee and lard, cheese, cream, chocolate, biscuits and cakes, palm and coconut oil.
nutrition-help-unsaturated-fat-what = Fats found in fish, avocado and nuts, and in sunflower, soybean, rapeseed and olive oils.
nutrition-help-unsaturated-fat-use = Preferable to saturated fat (WHO).
nutrition-help-monounsaturated-fat-what = A kind of unsaturated fat.
nutrition-help-monounsaturated-fat-use = Helps protect the heart by keeping “good” HDL cholesterol while lowering “bad” LDL.
nutrition-help-monounsaturated-fat-where = Olive and rapeseed oils, avocados, almonds, Brazil nuts, peanuts.
nutrition-help-polyunsaturated-fat-what = A kind of unsaturated fat. There are two main types: omega-3 and omega-6.
nutrition-help-polyunsaturated-fat-use = The body cannot make them in some forms, so a small amount has to come from food.
nutrition-help-omega-3-what = A polyunsaturated fat, essential in the diet.
nutrition-help-omega-3-use = At least 2 portions of fish a week are advised, one of them oily fish.
nutrition-help-omega-3-where = Oily fish (herring, trout, sardines, salmon, mackerel), flaxseed and rapeseed oils, walnuts.
nutrition-help-omega-6-what = A polyunsaturated fat, essential in the diet.
nutrition-help-omega-6-use = Most people get enough of it.
nutrition-help-omega-6-where = Rapeseed, corn and sunflower oils, walnuts, almonds, cashews.
nutrition-help-trans-fat-what = Unsaturated fatty acids of industrial or natural origin.
nutrition-help-trans-fat-use = Like saturated fats they raise cholesterol. WHO: less than 1 % of the energy.
nutrition-help-trans-fat-where = Margarine, shortening and fried foods; naturally in meat and dairy from ruminants.
nutrition-help-cholesterol-what = A fatty substance mostly made by the body in the liver.
nutrition-help-cholesterol-use = Saturated and trans fats raise the “bad” LDL cholesterol in the blood.
nutrition-help-carbohydrates-what = Sugars, starches and, depending on the standard, fibre.
nutrition-help-carbohydrates-use = The main source of energy of the body (WHO): 4 kcal per gram.
nutrition-help-sugars-what = All the mono- and disaccharides in a food, polyols excluded, whether natural or added. Free sugars, the ones the WHO limits, are those added to foods and drinks, plus those of honey, syrups and juices.
nutrition-help-sugars-use = Too much makes you gain weight and causes tooth decay. WHO: free sugars under 10 % of the energy, about 50 g.
nutrition-help-sugars-where = Sweets, cakes, biscuits, chocolate, fizzy drinks, breakfast cereals.
nutrition-help-starch-what = The carbohydrate of starchy foods, our main source of carbohydrate.
nutrition-help-starch-use = Starchy foods should make up just over a third of what you eat.
nutrition-help-starch-where = Potatoes, bread, rice, pasta, cereals.
nutrition-help-fibers-what = Substances found in the cell walls of vegetables, fruit, pulses and cereal grains.
nutrition-help-fibers-use = They help digestion and prevent constipation. WHO: at least 25 g a day (30 g in the UK).
nutrition-help-fibers-where = Wholemeal bread and pasta, brown rice, potatoes with their skins, pulses, vegetables, fruit, nuts and seeds.
nutrition-help-proteins-what = The building blocks of the body, such as muscle.
nutrition-help-proteins-use = 10 to 15 % of the energy is generally enough for adults (WHO).
nutrition-help-salt-what = Table salt, sodium chloride. Salt = sodium × 2.5: 1 g of salt holds 400 mg of sodium.
nutrition-help-salt-use = Too much raises blood pressure and the risk of heart attack and stroke. WHO: less than 5 g a day.
nutrition-help-salt-where = Bread, breakfast cereals, meat products, ready meals, sauces, cheese.
nutrition-help-sodium-what = The mineral of table salt: 1 g of salt holds 400 mg of sodium.
nutrition-help-sodium-use = WHO: less than 2000 mg of sodium a day.
nutrition-help-added-sugars-what = Sugars added to food or drink, plus the sugars in honey, syrups and unsweetened fruit juices: together they are called free sugars.
nutrition-help-added-sugars-use = UK advice: free sugars should make up no more than 5% of the energy you get from food and drink each day.
nutrition-help-added-sugars-where = Biscuits, chocolate, flavoured yoghurts, breakfast cereals, sweets, cakes and fizzy drinks.
nutrition-help-added-sugars-source = NHS, How does sugar in our diet affect our health? (2023)
nutrition-help-polyols-what = Sweeteners such as sorbitol, xylitol and erythritol; some fruit and vegetables also contain polyols naturally.
nutrition-help-polyols-use = Used instead of sugar to sweeten food. In large amounts they can have a laxative effect.
nutrition-help-polyols-where = Sugar-free drinks, desserts, ready meals, cakes, chewing gum and toothpaste.
nutrition-help-polyols-source = NHS, The truth about sweeteners (2023)
nutrition-help-chloride-what = A mineral; with sodium it makes up salt (sodium chloride).
nutrition-help-chloride-use = With sodium, it helps keep the level of fluids in the body balanced. Chloride also helps the body digest food.
nutrition-help-chloride-where = Salt, found naturally at low levels in all foods and added to many processed foods such as ready meals, bacon, cheese and savoury snacks.
nutrition-help-chloride-source = NHS, Vitamins and minerals: Others (2020)
nutrition-help-fluoride-what = A natural mineral.
nutrition-help-fluoride-use = It helps repair early damage to tooth enamel and makes it more resistant to tooth decay.
nutrition-help-fluoride-where = Tea, fish, some water supplies and most toothpaste.
nutrition-help-fluoride-source = Oral Health Foundation, Fluoride
nutrition-help-calcium-what = A mineral.
nutrition-help-calcium-use = Helps build bones and keep teeth healthy, regulates muscle contractions, including the heartbeat, and makes sure blood clots normally.
nutrition-help-calcium-where = Milk, cheese and other dairy foods, green leafy vegetables such as kale, soya drinks with added calcium, fortified bread and fish eaten with their bones.
nutrition-help-calcium-source = NHS, Calcium (2020)
nutrition-help-phosphorus-what = A mineral.
nutrition-help-phosphorus-use = Helps build strong bones and teeth, and helps release energy from food.
nutrition-help-phosphorus-where = Red meat, dairy foods, fish, poultry, bread, brown rice and oats.
nutrition-help-phosphorus-source = NHS, Vitamins and minerals: Others (2020)
nutrition-help-magnesium-what = A mineral.
nutrition-help-magnesium-use = Helps turn food into energy and keeps the parathyroid glands, which make hormones important for bone health, working normally.
nutrition-help-magnesium-where = Spinach, nuts and wholemeal bread.
nutrition-help-magnesium-source = NHS, Vitamins and minerals: Others (2020)
nutrition-help-potassium-what = A mineral.
nutrition-help-potassium-use = Helps control the balance of fluids in the body and helps the heart muscle work properly.
nutrition-help-potassium-where = Bananas, broccoli, parsnips, Brussels sprouts, beans and pulses, nuts and seeds, fish and meat.
nutrition-help-potassium-source = NHS, Vitamins and minerals: Others (2020)
nutrition-help-iron-what = A mineral.
nutrition-help-iron-use = Important for making red blood cells, which carry oxygen around the body.
nutrition-help-iron-where = Red meat, liver, beans such as red kidney beans and chickpeas, nuts, dried apricots and fortified breakfast cereals.
nutrition-help-iron-source = NHS, Iron (2020)
nutrition-help-zinc-what = A mineral.
nutrition-help-zinc-use = Helps make new cells and enzymes, process carbohydrate, fat and protein in food, and heal wounds.
nutrition-help-zinc-where = Meat, shellfish, dairy foods such as cheese, bread and cereal products such as wheatgerm.
nutrition-help-zinc-source = NHS, Vitamins and minerals: Others (2020)
nutrition-help-copper-what = A mineral.
nutrition-help-copper-use = Helps produce red and white blood cells and release the iron used to form haemoglobin, which carries oxygen around the body.
nutrition-help-copper-where = Nuts, shellfish and offal.
nutrition-help-copper-source = NHS, Vitamins and minerals: Others (2020)
nutrition-help-manganese-what = A mineral.
nutrition-help-manganese-use = Helps make and activate some enzymes, the proteins that carry out chemical reactions such as breaking down food.
nutrition-help-manganese-where = Bread, nuts, wholegrain breakfast cereals and green vegetables such as peas.
nutrition-help-manganese-source = NHS, Vitamins and minerals: Others (2020)
nutrition-help-selenium-what = A mineral.
nutrition-help-selenium-use = Helps the immune system work properly, helps reproduction and helps prevent damage to cells and tissues.
nutrition-help-selenium-where = Brazil nuts, fish, meat and eggs.
nutrition-help-selenium-source = NHS, Vitamins and minerals: Others (2020)
nutrition-help-chromium-what = A mineral needed in very small amounts.
nutrition-help-chromium-where = Meat, nuts and cereal grains.
nutrition-help-chromium-source = NHS, Vitamins and minerals: Others (2020)
nutrition-help-molybdenum-what = A mineral.
nutrition-help-molybdenum-use = Helps make and activate some of the enzymes involved in repairing and making genetic material.
nutrition-help-molybdenum-where = A wide variety of foods; foods that grow above ground tend to have more than those that grow below it, such as potatoes or carrots.
nutrition-help-molybdenum-source = NHS, Vitamins and minerals: Others (2020)
nutrition-help-iodine-what = A mineral.
nutrition-help-iodine-use = Helps make thyroid hormones, which keep cells and the metabolic rate healthy.
nutrition-help-iodine-where = Cows' milk and dairy products, eggs, sea fish and shellfish; cereals too, depending on the iodine in the soil.
nutrition-help-iodine-source = NHS, Iodine (2020)
nutrition-help-vitamin-a-what = Also known as retinol.
nutrition-help-vitamin-a-use = Helps the immune system work properly, helps vision in dim light and keeps skin and the lining of the nose healthy.
nutrition-help-vitamin-a-where = Cheese, eggs, oily fish, fortified low-fat spreads, milk, yoghurt and liver. Beta-carotene from orange and green vegetables is turned into it in the body.
nutrition-help-vitamin-a-source = NHS, Vitamin A (2020)
nutrition-help-vitamin-b1-what = Thiamin, also known as vitamin B1.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b1-use = Helps the body break down and release energy from food and keeps the nervous system healthy.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b1-where = Peas, bananas and oranges, nuts, wholegrain bread and fortified breakfast cereals.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b1-source = NHS, B vitamins and folic acid (2020)
nutrition-help-vitamin-b2-what = Riboflavin, also known as vitamin B2.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b2-use = Keeps skin, eyes and the nervous system healthy and helps the body release energy from food.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b2-where = Milk, eggs, fortified breakfast cereals, mushrooms and plain yoghurt.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b2-source = NHS, B vitamins and folic acid (2020)
nutrition-help-vitamin-b3-what = Niacin, also known as vitamin B3.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b3-use = Helps the body release energy from food and keeps the nervous system and skin healthy.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b3-where = Meat, fish, wheat flour and eggs.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b3-source = NHS, B vitamins and folic acid (2020)
nutrition-help-vitamin-b5-what = Pantothenic acid, also known as vitamin B5.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b5-use = Helps the body release energy from food, among other functions.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b5-where = Almost all vegetables, wholegrain foods and meats; chicken, beef, liver, eggs, mushrooms and avocado are good sources.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b5-source = NHS, B vitamins and folic acid (2020)
nutrition-help-vitamin-b6-what = Also known as pyridoxine.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b6-use = Helps the body use and store energy from protein and carbohydrate, and form haemoglobin, the substance in red blood cells that carries oxygen.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b6-where = Pork, poultry, some fish, peanuts, soya beans, wheatgerm, oats, bananas and milk.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b6-source = NHS, B vitamins and folic acid (2020)
nutrition-help-vitamin-b8-what = Biotin, also known as vitamin B7.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b8-use = Needed in very small amounts to help the body make fatty acids.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b8-where = A wide range of foods, but only at very low levels; bacteria in the bowel also make it.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b8-source = NHS, B vitamins and folic acid (2020)
nutrition-help-vitamin-b9-what = Folate, also known as vitamin B9; its manmade form is called folic acid.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b9-use = Helps the body form healthy red blood cells and reduces the risk of neural tube defects, such as spina bifida, in unborn babies.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b9-where = Broccoli, Brussels sprouts, leafy green vegetables, peas, chickpeas, kidney beans and breakfast cereals fortified with folic acid.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b9-source = NHS, B vitamins and folic acid (2020)
nutrition-help-vitamin-b12-what = A B vitamin.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b12-use = Helps the body make red blood cells, keep the nervous system healthy, release energy from food and use folate.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b12-where = Meat, fish, milk, cheese, eggs and some fortified breakfast cereals. It is not found naturally in fruit, vegetables and grains, so vegans may not get enough.
nutrition-help-vitamin-b12-source = NHS, B vitamins and folic acid (2020)
nutrition-help-vitamin-c-what = Also known as ascorbic acid.
nutrition-help-vitamin-c-use = Helps protect cells and keep them healthy, keeps skin, blood vessels, bones and cartilage healthy, and helps wound healing.
nutrition-help-vitamin-c-where = Citrus fruit, peppers, strawberries, blackcurrants, broccoli, Brussels sprouts and potatoes.
nutrition-help-vitamin-c-source = NHS, Vitamin C (2020)
nutrition-help-vitamin-d-what = The body makes it from direct sunlight on the skin.
nutrition-help-vitamin-d-use = Helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body, which are needed to keep bones, teeth and muscles healthy.
nutrition-help-vitamin-d-where = Sunlight on the skin, oily fish, red meat, egg yolks and fortified foods such as some fat spreads and breakfast cereals.
nutrition-help-vitamin-d-source = NHS, Vitamin D (2020)
nutrition-help-vitamin-e-what = A group of compounds.
nutrition-help-vitamin-e-use = Helps maintain healthy skin and eyes and strengthens the body's natural defence against illness and infection.
nutrition-help-vitamin-e-where = Plant oils such as rapeseed, sunflower, soya, corn and olive oil, nuts and seeds, and wheatgerm.
nutrition-help-vitamin-e-source = NHS, Vitamin E (2020)
nutrition-help-vitamin-k-what = A group of vitamins.
nutrition-help-vitamin-k-use = Needed for blood clotting, which helps wounds heal; there is also some evidence it helps keep bones healthy.
nutrition-help-vitamin-k-where = Green leafy vegetables such as broccoli and spinach, vegetable oils and cereal grains; small amounts in meat and dairy foods.
nutrition-help-vitamin-k-source = NHS, Vitamin K (2020)

# Cooking mode note card
recipe-cooking-note = Note
